          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Work over the rational function field $K=\\mathbb Q(q,t)$, where $q$ and $t$ are algebraically independent indeterminates. The rank-one reduced root system is $\\Phi=\\{\\pm\\alpha\\}$ with $\\alpha=2\\rho$, weight lattice $P=\\mathbb Z\\rho$, coroot lattice $Q^\\vee=\\mathbb Z\\alpha^\\vee$, and pairing $\\langle\\rho,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=1$. Put $z=X^\\rho$, so the lattice algebra is $K[z,z^{-1}]$; the finite Weyl reflection is $s_1(z)=z^{-1}$.\n\nLet $\\delta$ be the affine degree with $z^\\delta=q^{-2}$, and set $\\alpha_0=-2\\rho+\\delta$. The affine reflections and the length-zero symmetry act on Laurent functions by\n\n$$\ns_0 f(z)=f(q^{-2}z^{-1}),\\qquad s_1 f(z)=f(z^{-1}),\\qquad \\pi f(z)=f(q^{-1}z^{-1}).\n$$\n\nThus $s_0^2=s_1^2=\\pi^2=1$, $\\pi s_0\\pi=s_1$, and the affine denominators are $u_0=z^{-\\alpha_0}=q^2z^2$ and $u_1=z^{-\\alpha_1}=z^{-2}$. The affine Coxeter group generated by $s_0,s_1$ is infinite dihedral, with no finite braid relation between these reflections. The extended affine group is $W_{\\mathrm{aff}}\\rtimes\\Omega$, where $\\Omega=\\{1,\\pi\\}$ acts by swapping $s_0,s_1$; every element has a unique form $w\\pi^\\epsilon$ with $w$ an alternating reduced word in $s_0,s_1$ and $\\epsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}$.\n\nFor $m\\in\\mathbb Z$, define the Laurent polynomials $B_{1,m},B_{0,m}$ by\n\n$$\nB_{1,0}=B_{0,0}=0,\\quad B_{1,m}=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}z^{m-2j}\\ (m>0),\\quad B_{1,-m}=-B_{1,m}\\ (m>0),\n$$\n\n$$\nB_{0,m}=-\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}q^{-2j-2}z^{m-2-2j}\\ (m>0),\\qquad B_{0,-m}=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}q^{2j}z^{-m+2j}\\ (m>0).\n$$\n\nThey are the finite Laurent-polynomial versions of the Bernstein quotients with $s_1(z^m)=z^{-m}$ and $s_0(z^m)=q^{-2m}z^{-m}$. The symmetric Hecke normalization uses the same nonzero parameter $t$ at both affine simple roots: $(T_i-t)(T_i+t^{-1})=0$. The DAHA parameter $q$ is the lattice-translation parameter; it is distinct from the finite Hecke parameter $t$ and from the multiplicative type-A normalization $Q$.",
