          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-hh-free-associative-ring-and-relations-descent",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $S$ a set. There is a unital associative $R$-algebra $R\\langle S\\rangle$, the **free associative $R$-algebra on $S$**, whose underlying $R$-module is free on the set of finite words in $S$ (including the empty word), with product given on words by concatenation and extended $R$-bilinearly.\n\n1. **Universal property.** For every $R$-algebra $A$ ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and every map $S\\to A$ there is a unique unital $R$-algebra homomorphism $R\\langle S\\rangle\\to A$ extending it. When $R=k$ is a field and $S$ indexes a basis of a vector space $V$, $R\\langle S\\rangle$ reproduces the published tensor algebra $T(V)$ ([[def-tensor-algebra-of-a-vector-space]], [[thm-universal-property-of-the-tensor-algebra]]).\n2. **Two-sided ideal description.** For $E\\subseteq R\\langle S\\rangle$ the two-sided ideal $(E)$ ([[def-generated-and-principal-ideals]]) is the set of finite sums $\\sum_sa_se_sb_s$ with $a_s,b_s\\in R\\langle S\\rangle$ and $e_s\\in E$.\n3. **Quotient universal property.** If $\\varphi:R\\langle S\\rangle\\to A$ is an $R$-algebra homomorphism killing $E$, then $\\varphi$ factors uniquely through the quotient $R\\langle S\\rangle/(E)$ ([[thm-quotient-ring-universal-property]]); the quotient is the presented $R$-algebra with generators $S$ and relations $E$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-daha-a1-polynomial-representation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **polynomial representation** is the action of $\\mathcal H_{q,t}(A_1)$ on $\\mathcal P=K[z,z^{-1}]$ specified by sending $Z$ to multiplication by $z$, $Z^{-1}$ to multiplication by $z^{-1}$, $\\pi$ to $f(z)\\mapsto f(q^{-1}z^{-1})$, and $T_i$ to\n\n$$\n(\\mathsf T_i f)(z)=t f(s_i z)+(t-t^{-1})\\frac{f(s_i z)-f(z)}{z^{-\\alpha_i}-1},\\qquad i=0,1,\n$$\n\nwith $s_1z=z^{-1}$, $s_0z=q^{-2}z^{-1}$, $z^{-\\alpha_1}=z^{-2}$, and $z^{-\\alpha_0}=q^2z^2$. The quotient is interpreted by the finite Laurent sums in [[lem-daha-a1-bernstein-quotients-are-laurent]]. Well-definedness as an algebra action is proved in [[lem-daha-a1-polynomial-operators-satisfy-presentation]].",
